WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.509
A Trie, or prefix tree, stores a dictionary of strings by merging common prefixes along paths from a single root.

00:00:08.509 --> 00:00:17.620
Using parallel flat arrays of integer node indices instead of Python class objects keeps memory compact and lookups fast.

00:00:17.620 --> 00:00:25.000
Stepping through a word of length L takes at most L array accesses independent of dictionary size.

00:00:25.292 --> 00:00:33.994
Increment a pass counter on every node visited during insertion to know immediately how many dictionary words share that prefix.

00:00:33.994 --> 00:00:40.113
Mark an end counter at the final node to distinguish complete words from partial prefixes.

00:00:40.113 --> 00:00:45.892
Prefix autocomplete and frequency queries then finish in a single walk down the word.

00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:57.067
Treating thirty-bit or sixty-bit integers as fixed-length binary strings from most significant bit to least significant bit creates a Binary Trie.

00:00:57.067 --> 00:01:01.546
Each node has only two child slots for bit zero and bit one.

00:01:01.546 --> 00:01:09.087
You can insert numbers, delete numbers via pass counts, and query bitwise relationships in O(B) time.

00:01:09.375 --> 00:01:17.245
To find which number in the set maximizes XOR with a query x, walk down the Binary Trie from the highest bit to the lowest bit.

00:01:17.245 --> 00:01:24.000
At each bit position, greedily step into the child holding the opposite bit of x whenever that branch exists.

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.586
Securing a one in a higher bit position outweighs all lower bits combined.
